    $astroTide_master$> Draw a circle around the Sun at the distance where a rock would sit at the temperature of liquid water, and you have drawn the habitable zone — a thin annulus, warmed by starlight, that in our system barely reaches past Mars. Everything in this chapter lives far outside it, in the cold beyond the giant planets, where sunlight is a four-hundredth of what falls on Earth and a surface exposed to the sky freezes hard as granite. And yet some of these worlds are the warmest, most geologically violent places in the solar system. One of them throws molten sulfur three hundred kilometres into space. Another keeps an ocean of liquid water beneath its ice for billions of years. The energy doing this does not come from the Sun, and it does not come from radioactivity, and it does not come from leftover heat of formation. It comes from *motion* — from the slow, relentless gravitational kneading of a moon by the planet it circles, converted to heat by internal friction. Tidal heating is a second sun, invisible and unmarked on any map of the habitable zone, and it is the single reason the moons of the giants are alive rather than dead. The rest of this chapter is an argument that where a world sits relative to the Sun may matter far less than how its orbit is being flexed.

## The problem sunlight leaves behind

The framework that carried us through the terrestrial planets was built on two levers: **size and distance.** Size sets how long a world holds its internal heat — small worlds cool fast, lose their dynamos, lose their air. Distance sets the temperature of the surface, and temperature decides whether water is vapour, liquid, or ice. Draw the line where a grey body absorbs and re-radiates sunlight at 273 K and you get the classical **habitable zone**, the region where an Earth-like planet could hold liquid water on its surface. In our system that zone is narrow and it is near: roughly 0.95 to 1.7 astronomical units, give or take a model.

Jupiter orbits at 5.2 astronomical units. The sunlight there is weaker by a factor of $5.2^2 \approx 27$. At Saturn, 9.6 astronomical units out, it is weaker by nearly a hundred. The equilibrium temperature of an airless body at Jupiter's distance is about 110 K; at Saturn's, about 80 K. These are temperatures at which water ice is a structural mineral, as rigid and permanent as basalt. By every rule the earlier chapters established, the moons of the giants should be exactly what they look like at first glance: frozen, cratered, geologically finished — worlds that used up whatever formation heat they had and went cold a very long time ago.

Most of them are. Callisto, the outermost large moon of Jupiter, is precisely this: a battered ball of ice and rock, its surface a saturated field of craters four billion years old, a world that has done essentially nothing since it formed. It is the control case, the moon that obeys the rules.

And then, a few hundred thousand kilometres closer to Jupiter, there is Io.

{{image: Io (moon) | Io, innermost of Jupiter's large moons and the most volcanically active body known, resurfacing itself faster than impacts can scar it. Nothing about its distance from the Sun predicts this; the energy is tidal.}}

Io has no impact craters. Not few — **none** that anyone has found, on a surface exposed to the same four billion years of bombardment that saturated Callisto next door. It is painted in sulfurous yellows, whites, and reds, dotted with more than four hundred active volcanic centres, some erupting plumes that rise hundreds of kilometres above the surface into the vacuum. Its interior is partly molten. It radiates something like $10^{14}$ watts of internal heat, giving it a surface heat flux more than twenty times Earth's — from a body about the size of our Moon, at a distance where the Sun is a bright star and nothing more. Io is the most volcanically active object in the solar system, and by the logic of size-and-distance it has no right to exist.

The resolution is the subject of this chapter, and it is worth stating the punchline before building the argument, because it reorganises how you think about where life's energy can come from. **Io is being heated by being squeezed.** Not by the Sun, not by radioactive decay, not by leftover warmth, but by the mechanical work done on it, over and over, every forty-two hours, as it swings around Jupiter on an orbit that will not quite let it rest.

## Why a flexed body gets hot

Everyone knows tides raise the sea. The less familiar and more important fact is that tides raise the *solid body* too — and that a solid tide, worked hard enough, is a furnace.

Start with the geometry. Jupiter's gravity does not pull equally on every part of Io. The near side, closer to Jupiter, is pulled harder than Io's centre; the far side is pulled less. The difference — the *tidal force* — stretches Io along the Jupiter–Io line into a slightly elongated shape, a **tidal bulge** raised on both the near and far sides. This is not a metaphor and it is not only about oceans: the rock and ice of the moon itself deform, rising and falling by tens or hundreds of metres. For Io the solid-body tidal bulge is roughly a hundred metres high. The whole moon is a slightly squashed ellipsoid, its long axis aimed at Jupiter.

Now, crucially: **if that were the whole story, there would be no heat.** A moon in a perfectly circular orbit, rotating synchronously — keeping the same face toward its planet, as almost all large moons do — carries a tidal bulge that never changes. Same height, same orientation, forever. The rock is deformed, but it is *statically* deformed; nothing flexes, no work is done cycle to cycle, and a body that is not being repeatedly worked cannot be heated by tides. A circular orbit is a thermal dead end.

Heat requires that the bulge *change* over the course of each orbit, and change is exactly what an **eccentric** orbit provides — in two distinct ways at once.

**The bulge changes height.** On an eccentric orbit the moon's distance from the planet varies. At perijove (closest approach) the tidal force is stronger and the bulge rises; at apojove (farthest) it relaxes. Since tidal force scales as the inverse cube of distance, even a modest eccentricity produces a large fractional swing in bulge amplitude over one orbit. The moon is inflated and deflated along its long axis, once per orbit, like a slow heartbeat.

**The bulge changes orientation.** This is the subtler effect, and it is where Kepler's second law earns its keep. A synchronously rotating moon spins at a *constant* rate — the average orbital rate. But by Kepler's law of equal areas, the moon's *actual* angular speed around the planet is not constant: it sweeps faster near perijove and slower near apojove. So the direction to the planet, as seen from the moon's fixed rotating frame, is not steady. It swings ahead of the sub-planet point, then behind it, oscillating east and west over each orbit — a motion called **optical** or **physical libration.** The tidal bulge, which always tries to point at the planet, therefore rocks back and forth relative to the body of the moon. The moon is being twisted about its short axis while it is being inflated and deflated along its long one.

Put the two together and you have a body that is squeezed, stretched, and sheared through a full cycle of deformation every single orbit. The material is elastic but not *perfectly* elastic. Real rock and ice are **anelastic**: when you deform them and let go, they do not return all the stored energy: some fraction is lost to internal friction — to the microscopic grinding of grain boundaries and the viscous flow of warm material — and that lost mechanical work comes out as heat. It is precisely the reason a paperclip flexed back and forth grows warm and then breaks: hysteresis in the stress–strain cycle, integrated over the whole volume of the moon, forty-two hours at a time, for four and a half billion years.

{{image: Tidal heating | The tidal bulge on a moon in an eccentric orbit changes both height and orientation over each orbit; the resulting cyclic deformation, dissipated by internal friction, is the heat source. A circular orbit produces a static bulge and no heating.}}

### The scaling, and what it demands

How much heat? The standard result for a synchronously rotating moon of radius $R$, on an orbit of semi-major axis $a$ and small eccentricity $e$ around a planet of mass $M_p$, is

$$\dot{E} \;=\; \frac{21}{2}\,\frac{k_2}{Q}\,\frac{G\,M_p^{2}\,R^{5}\,n\,e^{2}}{a^{6}}$$

where $n = \sqrt{G M_p / a^{3}}$ is the **mean motion** (the orbital angular frequency), $k_2$ is the second-degree **Love number** measuring how readily the body deforms, and $Q$ is the dimensionless **quality factor** measuring how *poorly* it dissipates — a high $Q$ means low friction, a nearly elastic body that loses little energy per cycle, and a low $Q$ means a lossy, dissipative one. Every factor in that formula is telling you something, so read it slowly.

Because $n$ hides a power of $a$, the expression can be rewritten in a form that lays the dependences bare:

$$\dot{E} \;\propto\; \frac{k_2}{Q}\,\frac{R^{5}\,e^{2}\,n^{5}}{G^{2}} \;\propto\; \frac{k_2}{Q}\,\frac{R^{5}\,e^{2}}{a^{15/2}}\,.$$

Four levers, and they are not gentle:

- **Radius, to the fifth power.** A moon twice the size, all else equal, heats thirty-two times as hard. Tidal heating is a large-body phenomenon in its raw scaling — which will make the small worlds that manage it, later in this chapter, all the more surprising.
- **Eccentricity, squared.** No eccentricity, no heat — this is the mathematical statement of the "circular orbit is a dead end" argument above. Double the eccentricity and quadruple the power. **Heating lives or dies on eccentricity.**
- **Distance, catastrophically.** Written with $n$, the dependence is a fifth power of the orbital frequency; written with $a$, it falls as roughly the *fifteen-halves* power of orbital distance. Move a moon twice as far out and its tidal heating drops by a factor of $2^{7.5} \approx 180$. This single exponent is why the moons of a giant planet form a steep gradient of activity from the inside out, and why "distance from the *planet*" replaces "distance from the *Sun*" as the variable that matters.
- **The quality factor, inversely.** A dissipative interior — a warm, partly molten, or fractured one — has a low $Q$ and heats hard. A cold, rigid, elastic interior has a high $Q$ and shrugs the flexing off. This introduces a feedback that runs through everything below: heating lowers $Q$, and lower $Q$ means more heating.

The formula is honest about one thing above all. **It needs $e$.** Set the eccentricity to zero and the whole engine shuts off. Hold that fact, because it is about to become a paradox.

## The eccentricity that should not exist

Here is the difficulty, and it is a real one — for decades it looked fatal.

The very same tidal friction that heats the moon also acts back on the *orbit*, and what it does to the orbit is **circularise it.** Dissipation removes energy from the system, and for a moon of fixed angular momentum the minimum-energy orbit at that angular momentum is a circle. So tides drain eccentricity. A lone moon, raised into an eccentric orbit and then left alone, will have its eccentricity damped smoothly toward zero — and as $e$ falls, the heating (going as $e^2$) falls faster still, until the orbit is round, the bulge is static, and the moon is cold. Forever.

And this is fast. The circularisation timescale for tidal dissipation scales as

$$\tau_e \;\sim\; \frac{Q}{k_2}\,\frac{M_m}{M_p}\left(\frac{a}{R}\right)^{5}\frac{1}{n}\,,$$

and when you put Io's numbers into it, you get an eccentricity that should decay in something on the order of ten million years — geologically, an eyeblink, a fraction of a percent of the solar system's age. By this reckoning Io ought to have circularised its orbit and gone as dead as Callisto within the first few million years after it formed. Its eccentricity today is 0.0041, small but stubbornly nonzero, and by the mathematics just written it has no business still being there. The heat we measure — that $10^{14}$ watts — requires an eccentricity that tidal damping should have erased a thousand times over.

State the paradox in its sharpest form, because the resolution is the intellectual centre of the chapter:

> **A tidally heated moon destroys the very thing that heats it. Sustained tidal heating is therefore impossible for an isolated moon. Something outside the moon must be continuously re-supplying its eccentricity — pumping it back in as fast as the tides drain it away.**

There is only one thing in a moon's environment with the reach to do that: **the other moons.**

## Resonance: the pump that never stops

The escape from the paradox is one of the most beautiful mechanisms in dynamics, and it was worked out — remarkably — before anyone knew Io was hot at all. In 1979, weeks before Voyager 1 arrived at Jupiter, Stanley Peale, Patrick Cassen, and Ray Reynolds published a prediction that Io should be undergoing "widespread and recurrent" volcanism, driven by tidal heating maintained by orbital resonance. Voyager photographed the erupting plumes days later. It stands as one of the cleanest theoretical predictions in planetary science: the physics said *this moon must be on fire*, and the moon was on fire.

The mechanism is **mean-motion resonance.** Look at the orbital periods of Jupiter's three inner large moons:

| Moon | Period (days) | Semi-major axis (km) |
|---|---|---|
| **Io** | 1.769 | 421,700 |
| **Europa** | 3.551 | 671,034 |
| **Ganymede** | 7.155 | 1,070,412 |

The periods stand in the ratio $1 : 2 : 4$, to within a fraction of a percent. Europa takes almost exactly twice as long to orbit as Io; Ganymede almost exactly twice as long as Europa. Equivalently, the orbital *frequencies* — the mean motions — stand as $4 : 2 : 1$: Io laps Jupiter four times for every once of Ganymede. This is the **Laplace resonance**, named for Pierre-Simon Laplace, who first analysed the relation two centuries before anyone could explain why it should have any physical consequence.

Why does a locked period ratio matter? Because it fixes the *geometry of encounters.* Consider Io and Europa. Because their periods are locked at $1:2$, they line up on the same side of Jupiter — reach **conjunction** — at the same place in their orbits every time. Io does not meet Europa at a random longitude that drifts around Jupiter over the years; it meets Europa at the *same* longitude, orbit after orbit after orbit. And at each of those repeated conjunctions, Europa gives Io a small gravitational tug in the same direction, at the same phase of Io's orbit.

A single tug is nothing. But a small periodic force, applied *always at the same orbital phase*, does not average away — it accumulates. This is nothing more than the physics of pushing a child on a swing: a feeble shove is useless unless it is timed to the swing's own rhythm, and a feeble shove *so* timed builds a large amplitude. The repeated, phase-locked kicks from Europa (and, further out, from Ganymede) pump energy into a particular direction of Io's orbital motion, and the specific thing they pump up is its **eccentricity.** The resonance forces $e$; the tides drain it; and the system settles into a steady state where the two balance. The eccentricity Io needs in order to be heated is not a fossil left over from formation — it is being *manufactured, in real time*, by the gravitational conversation among the moons.

{{image: Laplace resonance | The Laplace resonance of Io, Europa, and Ganymede: orbital periods locked at 1:2:4, so conjunctions recur at fixed orbital phases. The repeated, precisely timed gravitational tugs force the eccentricities that tidal friction would otherwise erase.}}

The resonance has an exact and elegant signature. Write the mean motions as $n_{\text{Io}}$, $n_{\text{Eu}}$, $n_{\text{Gan}}$. The Laplace relation states

$$n_{\text{Io}} - 3\,n_{\text{Eu}} + 2\,n_{\text{Gan}} \;=\; 0\,,$$

and it holds to extraordinary precision — the left-hand side is zero to about one part in $10^{5}$. Equivalently, in terms of the moons' longitudes $\lambda$, the combination $\lambda_{\text{Io}} - 3\lambda_{\text{Eu}} + 2\lambda_{\text{Gan}}$ stays locked near $180^{\circ}$, librating about that value with a small amplitude of a fraction of a degree. What that particular combination guarantees is a strict rule of choreography: **the three moons never meet at once.** Whenever Io and Europa are in conjunction, Ganymede is on the far side of Jupiter; the triple alignment that the raw $1:2:4$ ratio might seem to allow is precisely forbidden. The system is not merely commensurate — it is phase-locked into a configuration that keeps the kicks coherent.

This is the mechanism that makes the whole thing work. Without the resonance, each moon circularises and dies. With it, the moons are gravitationally coupled into a single machine that converts Jupiter's enormous rotational and orbital angular momentum, by way of forced eccentricity and solid-body friction, into heat inside the moons. The engine's fuel is not sunlight and not radioactivity. It is the orbital energy of a planetary system, tapped by a resonance.

## The heating gradient

Now combine the two ideas — the steep distance-dependence of the heating formula, and the resonance that supplies the eccentricity — and you get a *gradient*, a smooth falling-off of activity with distance from Jupiter that the moons display almost like a graph you could read with your eyes.

**Io**, innermost, deepest in Jupiter's tidal grip, with the shortest orbit and the largest mean motion, gets the most. Its tidal power is on the order of $10^{14}$ watts — roughly a hundred trillion watts, more than twice the entire internal heat output of the Earth, delivered to a body with a fortieth of Earth's surface area. The surface heat flux, better than 2 watts per square metre, is more than twenty times terrestrial. This is why Io is molten inside and erupting outside, why it has no craters (it repaves itself with fresh lava and sulfur faster than impacts can mark it, resurfacing on the order of a centimetre per year), and why it is the most geologically alive world we know. Its heat does not build gradually and release episodically as Earth's does; it is poured in continuously and radiated continuously through hundreds of open volcanic vents.

**Europa**, next out at 671,000 km, sits at more than 1.5 times Io's distance. The $a^{-15/2}$ factor alone knocks its heating down by a factor of order thirty relative to Io's, and its tidal power is on the order of $10^{12}$ watts — roughly a hundredth of Io's, and comparable to its own radiogenic heat. That is far too little to melt rock, but it is *exactly enough for what matters here*: it keeps a global ocean of liquid water, tens of kilometres deep, from freezing solid beneath an ice shell. Europa's surface is ice, young and nearly craterless and cracked into a chaos of ridges, but it is not erupting rock — it is a frozen skin over a sea. Tidal heat there is not a furnace but a thermostat, and the temperature it holds is the one that keeps water liquid.

{{image: Europa (moon) | Europa's young, fractured ice shell overlies a global ocean of liquid water kept from freezing by tidal heat — roughly a hundredth of Io's power, but placed where it holds water liquid rather than melting rock.}}

**Ganymede**, further still at 1.07 million kilometres and — significantly — the outermost moon *in* the resonance, gets less again, and it forces very little eccentricity of its own. It probably harbours a liquid layer sandwiched deep between ices of different phases, but its surface is ancient in large patches, grooved and cratered, geologically quiet. It is the largest moon in the solar system, larger than Mercury, with its own magnetic field — and it is nearly dead on the outside, because it sits too far out on the gradient for tides to do much.

**Callisto**, outermost of the four, at 1.88 million kilometres, is **not in the resonance at all.** Nothing pumps its eccentricity; its orbit is essentially circular; and its tidal heating is negligible. It is exactly the moon that the size-and-distance rules predict: a cold, saturated, four-billion-year-old cratered ball, the sibling that never joined the machine. That Callisto looks the way a moon at Jupiter's distance is "supposed" to look, while Io three moons inward looks nothing like it, is the whole argument compressed into a single comparison.

$$\text{Io} \;\gg\; \text{Europa} \;\gg\; \text{Ganymede} \;\gg\; \text{Callisto}$$

is not a ranking by size (Ganymede and Callisto are the two largest), nor by distance from the Sun (they are all essentially the same distance out). It is a ranking by *how hard each moon is being flexed* — which is to say, by depth in Jupiter's tidal field and by position in the resonance. Read that chain of four worlds from left to right and you are reading the tidal-heating formula in the field.

## The wrong moon is the warm one

If tidal heating were simply "bigger and closer wins," Saturn's moons would obediently rank by size and distance. They do not, and the way they fail to is the most instructive lesson in the whole subject.

Consider two of Saturn's inner moons. **Mimas** is the closer, at 185,540 km, and it is famous — a small, heavily cratered ball dominated by the enormous impact crater Herschel, a third of the moon's own diameter, which gives it an unmistakable resemblance to a certain fictional space station and has earned it the nickname "the Death Star moon." Mimas looks utterly, comprehensively *dead*: a saturated cratered surface, no resurfacing, no plumes, no sign of internal activity of any kind. It is the picture of a frozen relic.

**Enceladus** is farther out, at 238,040 km, and it is *smaller* — about 500 km across against Mimas's 400, but lower in density and, on the size-and-distance ledger, an even less promising candidate for activity than Mimas. Yet Enceladus is one of the most astonishing objects in the solar system. From four great fractures across its south pole — the "tiger stripes" — it erupts continuous plumes of water vapour and ice grains, jetting hundreds of kilometres into space, sourced from a **global subsurface ocean** of liquid, salty water beneath its ice. Cassini flew *through* those plumes and tasted them: water, salts, silica nanoparticles that imply hydrothermal activity on a rocky seafloor, and organic molecules. Enceladus radiates something on the order of $10^{10}$ watts from its south polar region — far more than a body that small has any right to produce.

{{image: Enceladus | Enceladus erupts plumes of water vapour and ice from a global subsurface ocean through fractures at its south pole. Smaller and farther out than dead-looking Mimas, it is warm — because it, not Mimas, sits in an eccentricity-forcing resonance with Dione.}}

So here is the paradox, laid out plainly: the moon that is closer and looks battered and ancient is (or long seemed) dead, while the moon that is smaller and farther out is erupting. **Size and distance, the levers that ran the terrestrial planets, get the answer exactly backwards at Saturn.** What went wrong?

Nothing went wrong — the missing variable is *eccentricity*, and eccentricity is set by resonance. Enceladus is locked in a $2:1$ mean-motion resonance with the larger moon **Dione** farther out (Dione's period, 2.74 days, is almost exactly twice Enceladus's 1.37 days), and that resonance forces Enceladus's eccentricity in exactly the way the Laplace resonance forces Io's. Enceladus has a pump. Mimas, despite being deeper in Saturn's tidal field, is not in an eccentricity-forcing resonance that heats it — it has no pump, so its orbit stays quiet, and quiet orbits are cold orbits. The tidal formula was right all along; the term that decides the contest is $e^2$, and $e$ is a property not of the moon's size but of the *company it keeps*.

The lesson sharpens further — and the received story turns out to be wrong even about Mimas. Precise tracking of Mimas's orbit and rotation by Cassini revealed a libration in its motion that is hard to explain with a solid interior, and the leading interpretation, argued in a 2024 analysis of the data, is that Mimas hides a **young subsurface ocean** of its own, perhaps 20 to 30 kilometres down, only a few tens of millions of years old — an ocean with *no surface expression whatsoever*, beneath that ancient battered crust. A "stealth" ocean under the Death Star. If it holds up, Mimas is not the dead control case after all but a world caught early, its interior perhaps only recently pushed into a heating episode by the slow evolution of its orbit — with the surface not yet showing the slightest sign of the sea beneath.

Take the general moral, because it is the through-line to every ocean-world chapter that follows. **You cannot predict a moon's activity from its size and its distance from the Sun, or even from its distance from its planet.** The state of its orbit — whether some resonance is forcing its eccentricity — and the details of its interior structure decide whether it is a furnace, a thermostat, or a stone. At Saturn the "wrong" moon is the warm one, and the moon that looks deadest of all may be quietly hiding a sea.

## Orbits are not forever

Tidal interaction does one more thing, and it reframes everything above as a *snapshot of a process in motion* rather than a permanent arrangement. Tides do not only dissipate energy — they **transfer angular momentum**, and that transfer makes orbits migrate.

The mechanism is closest to home. The Moon raises a tidal bulge on the Earth, but because the Earth rotates faster (once a day) than the Moon orbits (once a month), the Earth's rotation drags that bulge *ahead* of the Earth–Moon line. The leading bulge, slightly closer to the Moon than the trailing one, pulls the Moon forward along its orbit, adding angular momentum to the Moon's motion — and by Newton's third law the Moon pulls back on the bulge, braking the Earth's spin. The consequences are measurable: the Moon **recedes from Earth at about 3.8 centimetres per year**, a rate confirmed by bouncing laser pulses off the retroreflectors left by the Apollo astronauts, and Earth's day lengthens by roughly two milliseconds per century. Angular momentum lost from Earth's spin reappears in the Moon's ever-widening orbit. The same physics tidally locks rotation: the reason the Moon shows us one face, and the reason nearly every large moon of the giants keeps one face to its planet, is that tides long ago braked their spins into synchrony with their orbits.

Now apply that to a system of moons that are *also* resonantly coupled, and the picture comes alive. Jupiter's tides push the moons outward at different rates, and because the inner moons migrate differently from the outer ones, the moons can drift *into* resonances as their orbits expand, lock together, and later, if conditions change, drift back *out.* The Laplace resonance itself was very likely *assembled* this way — the moons captured into their $4:2:1$ lock by differential tidal migration early in Jovian history — and it may still be slowly evolving today. There is active debate over whether the resonance is currently tightening or loosening, and over whether Io's heating is steady or **episodic**, cycling over hundreds of millions of years as the balance between resonant forcing and tidal damping breathes in and out. The tidal-heat output we measure at Io right now may be a high point, a low point, or a mean — we are watching one frame of a very long film.

This matters for the ocean worlds directly. If tidal heating is episodic, then a moon's ocean can be a *transient* feature — frozen for an era, liquid for another, as the resonance state and the interior temperature drift. Mimas's proposed young ocean is exactly what such a history would produce: a world only recently switched on. The moons of the giants are not a fixed tableau. They are a slowly churning dynamical system, and their internal fires flicker on the timescale of the churning.

## Energy that a star did not provide

Step back and see what has been overturned.

The classical habitable zone is a statement about *sunlight*: it marks the belt around a star where a rocky planet's surface can hold liquid water using starlight as its heat source. It is a genuinely useful concept, and for surface life on a planet like ours it is the right one. But it quietly assumes that the star is the only furnace on offer — that the energy which keeps water liquid, and drives the geology, and provides the disequilibrium that life feeds on, must ultimately be photons from a sun.

Tidal heating breaks that assumption. It is an energy source with **nothing to do with sunlight** — powered instead by the orbital dynamics of a planetary system, delivered through gravity and friction, and entirely indifferent to how far the world sits from any star. It keeps Io molten and Europa's ocean liquid and Enceladus's plumes flowing at four and nine astronomical units from the Sun, in a cold where surface water is granite. And in doing so it relocates the interesting real estate. The places to look for liquid water in the outer solar system are not sunlit surfaces — there are none warm enough — but the dark oceans *beneath* the ice of tidally worked moons, sealed from space, heated from within, and in the case of Enceladus demonstrably in contact with a warm rocky floor.

This is the argument the rest of the outer-system chapters build on. The same tidal engine that heats these moons also, at Io, feeds the volcanoes whose sulfur and oxygen inflate Jupiter's colossal magnetosphere — the coupling that the magnetosphere chapter turns on. It is the reason Titan, warmed and worked at Saturn, can run a whole chemistry of liquid methane and hidden water. And it is the physical basis for the ocean-worlds programme — Europa Clipper, the plume-sampling ambitions at Enceladus — that treats subsurface seas billions of kilometres from the Sun as the solar system's most promising habitats.

The single sentence to carry out of this chapter is the one that would have sounded absurd to anyone who thought the habitable zone was the whole story:

> **Energy for life need not come from a star.** A moon flexed hard enough by its planet, kept eccentric by its neighbours, can hold an ocean liquid for billions of years in the dark — and worlds like that may be the rule in the galaxy, not the exception.

## Further reading

The standard modern treatment of how orbits, tides, and resonances interlock is a graduate text, but the physically-minded reader can profitably graze it: {{book: Carl D. Murray and Stanley F. Dermott | Solar System Dynamics | 1999}} develops mean-motion resonance, the Laplace relation, and tidal evolution from first principles and remains the reference on the subject. For the story of Io specifically — the pre-Voyager prediction, the Voyager confirmation, and the physics of the most volcanic world — the primary literature around Peale, Cassen, and Reynolds's 1979 paper is short, readable, and worth finding; it is one of the cleanest cases in planetary science of theory arriving before the data. The Cassini mission's Enceladus results, widely available in review form, are the place to follow the plume-and-ocean argument in detail, and the 2024 reanalysis of Mimas's libration is where the "stealth ocean" case is made.

- **Carl D. Murray and Stanley F. Dermott, *Solar System Dynamics* (1999).** The authoritative account of resonances, tides, and orbital evolution — technical, but the source for everything in this chapter about why the Laplace relation holds and how moons migrate into and out of resonance.

## Problems

*Constants and data you may need: $G = 6.674\times10^{-11}\ \text{N m}^2\text{kg}^{-2}$. Jupiter: $M_p = 1.898\times10^{27}$ kg. Saturn: $M_s = 5.683\times10^{26}$ kg. Io: $R = 1{,}821$ km, $a = 421{,}700$ km, $P = 1.769$ d, $e = 0.0041$. Europa: $R = 1{,}561$ km, $a = 671{,}034$ km, $P = 3.551$ d, $e = 0.0094$. Ganymede: $a = 1{,}070{,}412$ km, $P = 7.155$ d. Callisto: $a = 1{,}882{,}700$ km, $P = 16.69$ d. Enceladus: $P = 1.370$ d. Dione: $P = 2.737$ d. Earth's internal heat flux $\approx 0.087\ \text{W/m}^2$; Io's internal power $\approx 1\times10^{14}$ W. 1 day $= 86{,}400$ s. Problem 3 (why a lone moon cannot stay hot) and problem 5 (the resonance pump) are the conceptual core; problem 8 is the finest reasoning here; problem 9 has no settled answer and is meant to be argued.*

**1 — Why a circle is a dead end.** A moon in synchronous rotation on a perfectly circular orbit carries a tidal bulge. **(a)** Describe the bulge's height and orientation as a function of time over one orbit. **(b)** Using the idea that tidal heat comes from *cyclic* deformation, explain why this moon experiences no tidal heating despite being permanently deformed. **(c)** Eccentricity introduces two distinct time-varying effects on the bulge. Name and explain both, and say which one requires Kepler's second law.

**2 — Reading the formula.** The tidal heating rate can be written $\dot{E} \propto (k_2/Q)\,R^{5}e^{2}/a^{15/2}$. **(a)** By what factor does $\dot{E}$ change if you double the moon's radius, holding everything else fixed? **(b)** By what factor if you double its orbital distance? **(c)** By what factor if you double its eccentricity? **(d)** A colleague proposes that a large, distant moon on a very circular orbit is a good tidal-heating candidate "because it's big." Rank the three levers by how decisively they can override each other, and say why "big" is the weakest argument of the three.

**3 — The paradox of the lone moon (conceptual core).** Argue, in careful prose and without heavy algebra, why an *isolated* moon cannot sustain tidal heating over geological time. Your argument must connect three facts: (i) tidal heating requires nonzero eccentricity; (ii) tidal dissipation removes orbital energy; (iii) the minimum-energy orbit at fixed angular momentum is a circle. Conclude with what this implies must be true of any moon we actually find to be tidally hot today.

**4 — Checking the Laplace resonance.** Using the periods in the data: **(a)** Compute the ratios $P_{\text{Eu}}/P_{\text{Io}}$ and $P_{\text{Gan}}/P_{\text{Eu}}$. How close are they to 2? **(b)** Compute the three mean motions $n = 360^{\circ}/P$ in degrees per day, and evaluate the Laplace combination $n_{\text{Io}} - 3\,n_{\text{Eu}} + 2\,n_{\text{Gan}}$. **(c)** Do the same check for Callisto against Ganymede: is $P_{\text{Cal}}/P_{\text{Gan}}$ a low-integer ratio? What does the answer say about why Callisto is dead?

**5 — The swing (conceptual core).** Explain, using the analogy of pushing a child on a swing, how a resonance converts many tiny gravitational tugs from a neighbouring moon into a large, sustained forced eccentricity. Your answer must make clear **(a)** why the periodic-and-phase-locked nature of the tugs is essential, **(b)** what would happen to the accumulated effect if the period ratio were *not* a ratio of small integers, and **(c)** how this supplies exactly the missing ingredient that problem 3 showed an isolated moon lacks.

**6 — The gradient, quantified.** Treat Io and Europa as orbiting the same planet with the same $k_2/Q$, so that $\dot{E} \propto R^{5}e^{2}/a^{15/2}$. **(a)** Using the tabulated radii, eccentricities, and semi-major axes, compute the ratio $\dot{E}_{\text{Io}}/\dot{E}_{\text{Eu}}$ predicted by this scaling. **(b)** Observationally Io outproduces Europa by closer to a factor of 100. What interior property, left fixed in part (a), most plausibly accounts for the remaining discrepancy, and in which direction must it differ between the two moons? **(c)** Comment on the fact that Europa's eccentricity is *larger* than Io's, yet Io is far hotter. Which factor in the formula wins, and by how much?

**7 — Io's heat, in familiar units.** **(a)** Compute Io's surface area and its internal heat flux in W/m², using its radius and an internal power of $1\times10^{14}$ W. **(b)** Express that flux as a multiple of Earth's $0.087\ \text{W/m}^2$. **(c)** Earth's internal heat is largely radiogenic and Io's is almost entirely tidal; both are roughly the same *total* power ($\sim10^{14}$ W). Why, then, is Io's *flux* so much larger, and what surface consequence does that have?

**8 — Enceladus and Mimas (the finest reasoning).** Mimas is closer to Saturn than Enceladus and comparable in size, yet Mimas long looked geologically dead while Enceladus erupts. **(a)** Using the periods given, show that Enceladus is in a $2:1$ mean-motion resonance with Dione. **(b)** Explain why the resonance, not the size or the distance, is the variable that resolves the paradox — refer explicitly to the term in the heating formula that the resonance controls. **(c)** A 2024 analysis argues Mimas hides a *young* subsurface ocean with no surface expression. Explain how the tidal-evolution ideas of this chapter (migration into resonance, episodic heating) make a *recently switched-on* Mimas physically reasonable, and why the absence of surface features does not refute it.

**9 — Open problem: the reach of the tidal engine.** The classical habitable zone is defined by starlight. Tidal heating provides a star-independent way to keep water liquid. **(a)** Argue for or against the proposition that *most* of the liquid-water real estate in a typical galaxy is tidally heated subsurface ocean, not sunlit surface. **(b)** Identify the single measurement or discovery that would most sharply settle the question in (a), and say what each answer would imply. **(c)** For the specific case of Mimas's proposed stealth ocean: design an observation or calculation that would confirm or refute it, being explicit about what you would measure and what each interior model predicts.

## Worked answers

**1 — Why a circle is a dead end.** **(a)** On a circular, synchronous orbit the distance to the planet is constant, so the tidal force is constant and the bulge has **constant height**; and since the moon keeps one face to the planet, the bulge keeps **constant orientation**, permanently aligned with the planet–moon line. It is frozen in place. **(b)** Tidal heat is the mechanical work lost to internal friction over each deformation *cycle*. A bulge that never changes performs no cycle: the rock is strained, but statically, and a static strain does no repeated work and dissipates no energy. Heating requires the strain to vary in time, and here it does not. **(c)** Eccentricity produces (i) a **radial tide**: the moon's distance varies over the orbit, so the tidal force — and hence the bulge height — rises near perijove and falls near apojove, inflating and deflating the moon once per orbit; and (ii) a **librational (optical) tide**: because the moon rotates uniformly but, by **Kepler's second law**, moves around the planet non-uniformly (faster near perijove, slower near apojove), the direction to the planet oscillates east–west in the moon's frame, so the bulge rocks back and forth in orientation. Effect (ii) is the one that requires Kepler's second law; effect (i) needs only that distance varies.

**2 — Reading the formula.** **(a)** $R^5$: doubling $R$ multiplies $\dot E$ by $2^5 = \mathbf{32}$. **(b)** $a^{-15/2}$: doubling $a$ multiplies by $2^{-7.5} = 1/181 \approx \mathbf{0.0055}$ — a fall of about a factor of 180. **(c)** $e^2$: doubling $e$ multiplies by $2^2 = \mathbf{4}$. **(d)** Distance is by far the most decisive lever ($2^{7.5} \approx 180$ per doubling), radius next ($2^5 = 32$), eccentricity least *per doubling* ($2^2 = 4$) — but note eccentricity can range over orders of magnitude and can go to zero, which shuts the engine off entirely regardless of the other two. "Big" is the weakest single argument because a factor-of-two size advantage (32×) is wiped out by a mere factor-of-1.65 in distance ($1.65^{7.5}\approx 32$), and by *any* amount of extra distance beyond that. A large, distant moon on a near-circular orbit is a poor candidate: the $a^{-15/2}$ penalty and the $e^2$ requirement both work against it, and its size cannot rescue it. Distance and eccentricity dominate; size is a tie-breaker.

**3 — The paradox of the lone moon.** The argument is a short chain. First, tidal heating scales as $e^2$, so a moon with zero eccentricity produces exactly zero tidal heat — heating *requires* a non-circular orbit. Second, the same internal friction that produces the heat is paid for by orbital energy: dissipation drains energy from the orbit. Third, for a moon of fixed orbital angular momentum, the lowest-energy orbit available is the circle — so as tides remove energy, the orbit relaxes toward a circle, i.e. the eccentricity is *damped* toward zero. Put the three together: an isolated moon's heating consumes the very eccentricity that makes heating possible, so the process is self-extinguishing. It circularises on a timescale (for Io-like parameters, of order $10^7$ years) that is geologically brief, after which $e \to 0$, the bulge goes static, and the moon goes cold — permanently. Therefore **any moon we observe to be tidally hot today cannot be isolated.** Something external must be re-supplying its eccentricity as fast as tides drain it. The only candidate with sufficient reach is the gravity of other moons, acting through orbital resonance. Observed heat is thus indirect but conclusive evidence of a resonance.

**4 — Checking the Laplace resonance.** **(a)** $P_{\text{Eu}}/P_{\text{Io}} = 3.551/1.769 = \mathbf{2.007}$; $P_{\text{Gan}}/P_{\text{Eu}} = 7.155/3.551 = \mathbf{2.015}$. Both within about 1% of 2. **(b)** Mean motions: $n_{\text{Io}} = 360/1.769 = 203.5^{\circ}/\text{d}$; $n_{\text{Eu}} = 360/3.551 = 101.4^{\circ}/\text{d}$; $n_{\text{Gan}} = 360/7.155 = 50.3^{\circ}/\text{d}$. Then

$$n_{\text{Io}} - 3n_{\text{Eu}} + 2n_{\text{Gan}} = 203.5 - 3(101.4) + 2(50.3) = 203.5 - 304.2 + 100.6 = \mathbf{-0.1^{\circ}/\text{d}}\,,$$

i.e. essentially zero — to the precision of these rounded periods, indistinguishable from the exact relation $n_{\text{Io}} - 3n_{\text{Eu}} + 2n_{\text{Gan}} = 0$. **(c)** $P_{\text{Cal}}/P_{\text{Gan}} = 16.69/7.155 = \mathbf{2.33}$ — not a ratio of small integers, and not close to one. Callisto is therefore **not** locked in a mean-motion resonance with the inner three; nothing forces its eccentricity, its orbit stays nearly circular, and its tidal heating is negligible. That is precisely why Callisto, though the same size class and the same distance from the Sun as its siblings, is a cold cratered relic: it is outside the pump.

**5 — The swing.** **(a)** A child on a swing has a natural period; a push delivered *at the same phase every cycle* — always as the swing starts forward — adds a little energy each time, and those additions accumulate into a large amplitude, whereas the same total push delivered at random phases would sometimes add and sometimes subtract and would average to nothing. A resonance does exactly this for a moon: because the period ratio is locked, conjunctions with the neighbouring moon recur at the *same orbital phase*, so the small gravitational tug is delivered in phase orbit after orbit, and its effect on the eccentricity accumulates instead of averaging away. **(b)** If the period ratio were *not* a ratio of small integers, conjunctions would occur at a longitude that drifts steadily around the orbit; the tug would be applied at a slowly cycling phase, adding energy for a while and removing it for a while, so the long-term average would be near zero and no large forced eccentricity would build. Commensurability is what keeps the phase fixed. **(c)** Problem 3 showed an isolated moon has no way to replace the eccentricity that tides drain, so it goes cold. The resonance is that missing way: the phase-locked kicks *manufacture* eccentricity continuously, and the system settles at the value where resonant forcing balances tidal damping. The moon stays hot not because its eccentricity is a leftover, but because it is being resupplied in real time.

**6 — The gradient, quantified.** **(a)** With $\dot E \propto R^5 e^2 / a^{15/2}$, form the ratio:

$$\frac{\dot E_{\text{Io}}}{\dot E_{\text{Eu}}} = \left(\frac{R_{\text{Io}}}{R_{\text{Eu}}}\right)^{5}\left(\frac{e_{\text{Io}}}{e_{\text{Eu}}}\right)^{2}\left(\frac{a_{\text{Eu}}}{a_{\text{Io}}}\right)^{15/2}.$$

Radius: $(1821/1561)^5 = (1.167)^5 = 2.16$. Eccentricity: $(0.0041/0.0094)^2 = (0.436)^2 = 0.190$. Distance: $(671034/421700)^{7.5} = (1.591)^{7.5}$; $\ln 1.591 = 0.464$, $\times 7.5 = 3.48$, $e^{3.48} = 32.5$. Product: $2.16 \times 0.190 \times 32.5 \approx \mathbf{13}$. So this scaling alone predicts Io outproduces Europa by roughly a factor of 13. **(b)** The one interior property held fixed in part (a) is $k_2/Q$. Io is more dissipative — hotter, partly molten, lower $Q$ (and higher $k_2$) — so its $k_2/Q$ is larger than Europa's, and that difference pushes the true ratio from $\sim13$ up toward the observed $\sim100$. The needed factor of $\sim8$ in $k_2/Q$ is physically reasonable for a molten-versus-solid contrast, and it is a *feedback*: heating lowers $Q$, which raises heating. **(c)** Europa's eccentricity is larger (0.0094 vs 0.0041), which by itself *favours* Europa by a factor $(0.0094/0.0041)^2 = 5.3$. But the distance factor favours Io by 32.5 and the radius factor by 2.2, for a net $2.2 \times 32.5 / 5.3 \approx 13$ in Io's favour. The **distance term dominates decisively**: Io wins not by being more eccentric — it is less — but by being far deeper in Jupiter's tidal field. This is the quantitative content of "heating falls steeply with distance."

**7 — Io's heat, in familiar units.** **(a)** Surface area $= 4\pi R^2 = 4\pi (1.821\times10^6)^2 = 4\pi (3.316\times10^{12}) = 4.17\times10^{13}\ \text{m}^2$. Flux $= 10^{14}/4.17\times10^{13} = \mathbf{2.4\ \text{W/m}^2}$. **(b)** $2.4/0.087 = \mathbf{28}$ — about twenty-eight times Earth's internal heat flux. **(c)** The *total* powers are comparable ($\sim10^{14}$ W each), but Io's surface area is far smaller — Io's radius is under a third of Earth's, so its area is roughly a thirteenth of Earth's. The same total power spread over a much smaller surface gives a much higher flux per square metre. The surface consequence is dramatic: Io cannot conduct or store that flux the way Earth's thick lithosphere does, so it sheds heat through direct volcanism — hundreds of active vents, near-continuous eruption, and resurfacing at a centimetre per year that erases impact craters as fast as they form. High flux, small body: a world turned inside out.

**8 — Enceladus and Mimas.** **(a)** $P_{\text{Dione}}/P_{\text{Enc}} = 2.737/1.370 = \mathbf{1.998} \approx 2$. Enceladus completes almost exactly two orbits for each one of Dione's, the signature of a $2:1$ mean-motion resonance; conjunctions recur at a fixed phase and force Enceladus's eccentricity. **(b)** The heating formula goes as $e^2$, and eccentricity is set by resonance, not by size or distance. Enceladus, though small and farther out than Mimas, has a resonant pump (with Dione) that forces its eccentricity and keeps the $e^2$ term alive; Mimas, though deeper in Saturn's tidal field, lacks such an eccentricity-forcing resonance, so its $e$ stays low and its $e^2$ term is small. The contest is decided by the term the resonance controls — $e^2$ — which is exactly why the "wrong" (smaller, farther) moon is the warm one. **(c)** Tidal evolution migrates moons' orbits over time, so a moon can drift *into* a resonance long after formation, at which point its forced eccentricity — and its heating — switch on; heating can also be episodic as resonances tighten and loosen. A Mimas that entered (or is entering) a heating episode only tens of millions of years ago would have a *young* ocean, melted from within but not yet long enough to have cracked, resurfaced, or vented at the surface — heat diffuses and surfaces respond slowly. So the absence of surface features does not refute a subsurface ocean; on a recently switched-on world it is exactly what you would expect, and the ocean betrays itself instead through the moon's rotational libration, which a solid interior cannot reproduce.

**9 — Open problem: the reach of the tidal engine.** No settled answer; a strong response weighs the arguments. **(a)** *For the proposition:* the classical habitable zone around any star is a thin annulus and demands a rocky planet parked in it, whereas nearly every giant planet comes with a retinue of moons, and any moon caught in an eccentricity-forcing resonance can host a subsurface ocean regardless of stellar distance; giant planets and their moon systems are common, so tidally heated oceans could vastly outnumber sunlit habitable surfaces. *Against:* tidal oceans are sealed under ice, cut off from starlight and (mostly) from an atmosphere, so whatever life they might host is energy-poor and chemically limited compared to a sunlit biosphere; "real estate" measured by liquid-water *volume* may favour tidal oceans, but measured by *usable* free energy the sunlit surfaces may still dominate. The honest position notes that the question is partly about definitions — volume of liquid water versus available energy versus detectability. **(b)** The sharpest single discriminant would be a robust census of subsurface oceans among the moons of the solar system's own giants (how many, how thick, how long-lived), extended if possible to exomoons: if oceans prove near-universal among resonant moons, the tidal habitat is generic and the proposition strengthens; if they turn out rare or short-lived, sunlit surfaces reclaim the lead. A biosignature from *inside* one such ocean — organics plus a demonstrable free-energy source, as the Enceladus plume tantalisingly hints — would settle whether tidal oceans are merely wet or actually inhabited. **(c)** For Mimas specifically: the ocean hypothesis rests on the observed libration amplitude, which is larger than a rigid interior allows. The decisive test is a precise, independent determination of Mimas's interior — for example, a dedicated tracking campaign measuring the libration and the tidal Love number $k_2$ (an ocean-bearing Mimas is more deformable, higher $k_2$, than a solid one), combined with high-resolution gravity and topography to constrain the ice-shell thickness. A solid Mimas predicts small libration and low $k_2$; a Mimas with a 20–30 km-deep ocean predicts the larger observed libration and a distinctly higher $k_2$. Heat-flow measurements at the surface would help but may be null even for a real young ocean, since a recently melted interior need not yet leak detectable heat — so the cleanest test is the mechanical one: measure how much the moon flexes, and let the interior betray itself.$astroTide_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
