with q as (
  select $astroTide_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does a moon in a perfectly circular, synchronous orbit experience no tidal heating, even though it is permanently deformed into a tidal bulge?", "options": ["Because a circular orbit keeps the moon too far from its planet for the tidal force to raise any bulge in solid rock", "Because on a circular synchronous orbit the bulge has constant height and constant orientation, so the strain is static; tidal heat comes only from the mechanical work lost to internal friction over a repeating deformation cycle, and a bulge that never changes performs no cycle and dissipates nothing", "Because synchronous rotation cancels the tidal force exactly, leaving the rock unstrained at all times", "Because a circular orbit has zero orbital energy available to be converted into heat"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Tidal heat is the fraction of mechanical work lost to internal friction (anelastic hysteresis) integrated over each cycle of deformation. On a circular orbit the moon's distance to the planet never changes, so the bulge height is fixed; and because the moon keeps one face to the planet, the bulge orientation is fixed too. The rock is strained, but statically, and a static strain does no repeated work and loses no energy per cycle. Heating requires the bulge to CHANGE over each orbit, which only an eccentric orbit provides. This is the chapter's 'a circular orbit is a thermal dead end' point, and the mathematical version is that heating scales as e^2, vanishing at e=0. The other options are wrong: the tidal bulge is genuinely raised in the solid body (about 100 m high on Io); synchronous rotation does not cancel the tidal force; and orbital energy is plentiful, it simply cannot be tapped without a time-varying strain."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The tidal heating rate scales as E-dot proportional to (k2/Q) R^5 e^2 / a^(15/2). If you moved a moon from its orbit to twice the orbital distance, holding radius, eccentricity, and interior fixed, by roughly what factor would its tidal heating change?", "options": ["It would fall by a factor of about 180 (2^7.5), because the heating goes as roughly the fifteen-halves power of orbital distance", "It would fall by a factor of 4, because heating scales as the inverse square of distance like sunlight does", "It would fall by a factor of 32, because the dominant lever is the radius-to-the-fifth term", "It would be essentially unchanged, because distance only affects the surface temperature, not the internal flexing"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Because the mean motion n hides a power of a, the heating rate falls as a^(-15/2), so doubling the distance multiplies the heating by 2^(-7.5), a drop of about 180 times. This single catastrophic exponent is why 'distance from the planet' replaces 'distance from the Sun' as the variable that matters, and why the moons of a giant form a steep gradient of activity from the inside out. Distance is the most decisive of the four levers (about 180 per doubling), radius next (2^5 = 32 per doubling), and eccentricity least per doubling (2^2 = 4) though it can go to zero and shut the engine off entirely. It is emphatically NOT an inverse-square law like sunlight; the radius term is real but weaker than distance here; and distance drives the internal flexing directly through the tidal force, not just surface temperature."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "For decades the tidal-heating explanation of Io looked fatal because of a paradox. What is the paradox?", "options": ["Io radiates more heat than sunlight delivers to it, which appears to violate conservation of energy", "Io's interior is too cold and rigid (too high a quality factor Q) for tidal flexing to dissipate any measurable energy", "The same tidal friction that heats the moon also drains orbital energy, circularising the orbit and damping the eccentricity that heating requires; Io should have gone round and cold within about ten million years, yet it is still hot with e = 0.0041 today", "Io's orbit is already perfectly circular, so the heating formula predicts exactly zero output despite the observed volcanism"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Tidal dissipation removes orbital energy, and for a moon of fixed angular momentum the minimum-energy orbit is a circle, so tides damp eccentricity toward zero. Since heating goes as e^2, a tidally heated lone moon destroys the very thing that heats it, and the process is self-extinguishing. Plugging Io's numbers into the circularisation timescale gives decay in about 10^7 years, a geological eyeblink, after which the orbit would be round and the moon dead like Callisto. Yet Io still has e = 0.0041 and radiates about 10^14 W. The resolution is that the eccentricity is not a fossil but is being resupplied in real time by the Laplace resonance with Europa and Ganymede. The other options are wrong: energy is conserved (the source is orbital, not stellar); Io's interior is hot and dissipative (low Q, which heats hard); and Io's orbit is not circular, it is stubbornly and inexplicably eccentric, which is the whole puzzle."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How does the Laplace resonance of Io, Europa, and Ganymede sustain Io's eccentricity against tidal damping?", "options": ["The three moons collide gently at every conjunction, transferring momentum that stirs up Io's eccentricity", "Ganymede's magnetic field couples to Io's iron core and torques its orbit into an eccentric shape", "Jupiter's oblateness alone forces all three orbits to remain eccentric, independent of the moons' mutual gravity", "Their orbital periods are locked in a 1:2:4 ratio, so conjunctions recur at the same orbital phase every time; the repeated, phase-locked gravitational tugs from Europa and Ganymede accumulate rather than average away, pumping up Io's eccentricity like well-timed pushes on a swing"], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "The periods stand as 1:2:4 (Io 1.769 d, Europa 3.551 d, Ganymede 7.155 d), equivalently mean motions of 4:2:1, and the Laplace relation n_Io - 3 n_Eu + 2 n_Gan = 0 holds to about one part in 10^5. Because the ratio is locked, Io meets Europa at the SAME longitude orbit after orbit, so a small tug delivered always at the same orbital phase does not average to zero but accumulates, exactly like a feeble but well-timed push building a large swing amplitude. If the period ratio were not a ratio of small integers, conjunctions would drift around the orbit, the tug would sometimes add and sometimes subtract, and no forced eccentricity would build. The resonance manufactures the eccentricity in real time, balancing what tides drain. The libration of the angle keeps the triple alignment forbidden so the kicks stay coherent. The distractors are non-mechanisms: the moons never collide, and neither magnetic coupling nor Jupiter's oblateness is the eccentricity source described in the chapter."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "At Saturn, Mimas orbits closer (185,540 km) and is comparable in size to Enceladus (238,040 km, and actually smaller in mass), yet Mimas long looked dead while Enceladus erupts water plumes from a global ocean. What resolves this paradox?", "options": ["Enceladus is in a 2:1 mean-motion resonance with Dione that forces its eccentricity and keeps the e^2 heating term alive, whereas Mimas, though deeper in Saturn's tidal field, sits in no eccentricity-forcing resonance, so its e stays low and it is not heated", "Enceladus is made of much denser rock than Mimas, so it retained far more radiogenic heat from formation", "Mimas is shielded from Saturn's tidal force by the shadow of the main rings, which pass between it and the planet", "Enceladus is closer to the Sun than Mimas and so receives more sunlight to melt its ice"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The missing variable is eccentricity, and eccentricity is set by resonance, not by size or distance. Enceladus is locked in a 2:1 mean-motion resonance with the larger, farther moon Dione (Dione's 2.737 d is almost exactly twice Enceladus's 1.370 d), which forces Enceladus's eccentricity just as the Laplace resonance forces Io's, keeping the e^2 term in the heating formula alive; Enceladus radiates on the order of 10^10 W from its south pole. Mimas, despite being deeper in Saturn's tidal field, has no such pump, so its orbit stays quiet and cold. This is the chapter's central lesson that 'the wrong moon is the warm one' and that e is a property of the company a moon keeps, not its size. The distractors get the physics backwards: Enceladus is lower in density, not higher; the rings do not shield tidal gravity; and both moons are essentially the same (far) distance from the Sun, where sunlight cannot melt ice anyway. (Precise Cassini tracking later suggested even Mimas may hide a young 'stealth' ocean, sharpening the lesson further.)"}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Using the tidal-heating formula and the resonance mechanism, explain why the four Galilean moons form the activity gradient Io >> Europa >> Ganymede >> Callisto. Address what each of the four looks like, and make clear why this ordering is NOT a ranking by size or by distance from the Sun.", "rubric": "A strong answer identifies that the ranking is by how hard each moon is being flexed, which combines depth in Jupiter's tidal field (the steep a^(-15/2) distance dependence) with position in the Laplace resonance (which supplies the eccentricity, since heating goes as e^2). It should walk the four moons: (1) IO, innermost at 421,700 km with the largest mean motion, radiates about 10^14 W, a surface heat flux above 2 W/m^2 (more than 20x Earth's), is partly molten, has 400+ active volcanoes, no impact craters, and resurfaces about 1 cm/yr with lava and sulfur. (2) EUROPA, at 671,000 km (more than 1.5x Io's distance), gets roughly a hundredth of Io's power (order 10^12 W) because the a^(-15/2) factor alone cuts heating by about 30x; that is too little to melt rock but exactly enough to keep a global liquid-water ocean from freezing beneath a young, cracked, nearly craterless ice shell; tidal heat here is a thermostat, not a furnace. (3) GANYMEDE, at 1.07 million km and the outermost moon IN the resonance, forces little eccentricity of its own, gets less heating, and is largely ancient, grooved, and geologically quiet on the surface, though it is the largest moon in the solar system (bigger than Mercury) with its own magnetic field and probably a deep liquid layer. (4) CALLISTO, outermost at 1.88 million km, is NOT in the resonance at all, so nothing pumps its eccentricity; its orbit is essentially circular, its tidal heating negligible, and it is a cold, saturated, 4-billion-year-old cratered ball, the 'control case' that obeys the size-and-distance rules. The answer must explicitly note the ordering is not by size (Ganymede and Callisto are the two LARGEST yet coldest) nor by distance from the Sun (all four are essentially the same distance from the Sun); it is set by tidal flexing, i.e. distance from JUPITER plus resonant eccentricity forcing. A top answer notes that Callisto being 'what a moon at Jupiter's distance is supposed to look like' while Io three moons inward looks nothing like it compresses the whole argument into one comparison."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter argues that tidal heating overturns the assumption behind the classical habitable zone. Explain what the classical habitable zone assumes, how tidal heating breaks that assumption, and what this implies for where to look for liquid water in the outer solar system. Support your argument with the chapter's specifics.", "rubric": "A strong answer explains that the classical habitable zone is a statement about SUNLIGHT: it marks the thin annulus around a star (roughly 0.95 to 1.7 AU in our system) where a rocky planet's surface can hold liquid water using starlight as the heat source, and it quietly assumes the star is the only furnace on offer. It should state that tidal heating breaks this assumption because it is an energy source with nothing to do with sunlight: it is powered by the orbital dynamics of a planetary system (a moon flexed by its planet, kept eccentric by resonance with its neighbours), delivered through gravity and internal friction, and entirely indifferent to distance from any star. Concrete support should include that this engine keeps Io molten, Europa's ocean liquid, and Enceladus's plumes flowing at 4 to 9-plus AU from the Sun, in cold where surface water is as rigid as granite (equilibrium temperatures about 110 K at Jupiter, 80 K at Saturn), with sunlight a small fraction (about a 400th) of Earth's. The key implication: the interesting real estate is relocated. The places to look for liquid water in the outer solar system are not sunlit surfaces (none are warm enough) but the dark oceans BENEATH the ice of tidally worked moons, sealed from space, heated from within, and in the case of Enceladus demonstrably in contact with a warm rocky seafloor (Cassini tasted salts, silica nanoparticles implying hydrothermal activity, and organics in the plumes). A top answer closes with the chapter's thesis sentence: energy for life need not come from a star; a moon flexed hard enough by its planet and kept eccentric by its neighbours can hold an ocean liquid for billions of years in the dark, and such worlds may be the rule in the galaxy rather than the exception, which is the physical basis for the ocean-worlds programme (Europa Clipper, Enceladus plume sampling). Optionally it may note the honest caveat that these sealed oceans are energy-poor and cut off from starlight, so their comparison to sunlit biospheres is genuinely open."}]$astroTide_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/giant-planets/tidal-heating', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
